
In this directory are six fixed-grid files, fixed_grids_1 through
fixed_grids_6, which are used to test different grid configurations.
There is also a plot file (plt0000_1 through plt0000_6) for each
grid configuration so you can see the grid configurations.

This directory used to also contain inputs_* and probin_* files.
Those predated the C++ problem setup (Source/prob/prob_init.cpp) and no
longer ran, so they have been removed.  To use one of the grid
configurations, start from an existing 2D inputs file (for example
../regtest.2d.hotspot) and set

  amr.regrid_file = test_grids/fixed_grids_N

together with the level-0 domain, max_level and ref_ratio listed below.
Each fixed_grids file lists the grids for levels 1 and up; the level-0
grids come from amr.n_cell and amr.max_grid_size.

******************************************************************************

fixed_grids_1 :

amr.n_cell = 16 16, geometry.prob_hi = 1. 1., amr.max_level = 2,
amr.ref_ratio = 2 2, amr.max_grid_size = 8

4 level 0 grids, 2 level 1 grids, 2 level 2 grids.

This is the only case with multiple level 0 grids.  It also has
level 2 grid boundaries overlaying level 1 grid boundaries overlaying
level 0 grid boundaries.  None of the fine grids touch the physical
boundaries.

It was originally run in x-y with solid walls or periodic in the
x-direction, and in r-z with a solid wall in the r-direction, with a
solid wall in the low-y direction and outflow in the high-y direction.

******************************************************************************

fixed_grids_2 :

amr.n_cell = 16 16, geometry.prob_hi = 1. 1., amr.max_level = 2,
amr.ref_ratio = 4 2

1 level 0 grid, 2 level 1 grids, 3 level 2 grids

This is the only case set up to test inflow.  One of the level 1
grids and two of the level 2 grids touch the low-y inflow face. This
has a level 2 grid spanning a level 1 grid interface.

It was originally run in x-y or r-z, with solid walls in the
x-direction, a solid wall or inflow in the low-y direction and outflow
in the high-y direction.

******************************************************************************

fixed_grids_3 :

amr.n_cell = 16 16, geometry.prob_hi = 1. 1., amr.max_level = 2,
amr.ref_ratio = 4 2

1 level 0 grid, 3 level 1 grids, 3 level 2 grids

This should be run in x-y, periodic in the x-direction, with a solid
wall in the low-y direction and outflow in the high-y direction.  This
is especially useful for the periodic grid interfaces.

******************************************************************************

fixed_grids_4 :

amr.n_cell = 16 16, geometry.prob_hi = 2. 2., amr.max_level = 1,
amr.ref_ratio = 2

1 level 0 grid, 5 level 1 grids

This is doubly periodic, and is useful for testing periodic interfaces
as well as different types of corners (inside, outside, diagonal).

******************************************************************************

fixed_grids_5 :

amr.n_cell = 32 32, geometry.prob_hi = 2. 2., amr.max_level = 1,
amr.ref_ratio = 2

1 level 0 grid, 7 level 1 grids

This is doubly periodic, and is useful for testing periodic grid interfaces.

******************************************************************************

fixed_grids_6 :

amr.n_cell = 64 64, geometry.prob_hi = 2. 2., amr.max_level = 2,
amr.ref_ratio = 2 2

1 level 0 grid, 4 level 1 grids, 3 level 2 grids

It is periodic in the x-direction and has some nasty level 1/2 and
level 0/1 interfaces.  It is useful for testing periodic grid interfaces.

******************************************************************************
